WEBVTT

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:21.600
I'm on a journey to discover how a thousand years of history have shaped the way we built Britain.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:32.960
I'm heading west through a landscape that looks like the best of rural England, but which, in the 18th century, became home to the most fashionable buildings of the day.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.880
Here, elegant cities flourished.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:39.960
There was Bath - playground of the elite.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.920
And Dublin, after London, the grandest city in the kingdom.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:51.680
And here too the seeds of the industrial revolution were being sown.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.680
This is an age when Britain discovered a new sense of pride.

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:19.000
There's an optimism about the towns and cities we built, about the great houses and gardens, about the machines we invented, and the west embraced this revolution, turning itself from a country backwater into a leader of style and sophistication.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:23.320
The mysterious spires of Blenheim Palace in Oxfordshire.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:39.600
Blenheim was built by Queen Anne to celebrate the deeds of her most famous general, the Duke of Marlborough.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:46.160
The palace took its name from the battle of Blenheim in 1704.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:56.960
It marked Marlborough's resounding defeat of the French, a victory which ended their hopes of dominating Europe.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:03.480
This building was meant to leave no-one in doubt how glorious that victory was.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:11.280
It's built on a stupendous scale.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:32.600
Blenheim shows Britain with a new self-confidence, a bit of a swagger, a celebration in stone, shouting to the world, "We're top dog now and we plan to stay that way."

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:41.920
The style of Blenheim was already fashionable in Europe, but relatively new to Britain.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:47.840
It echoes the great civilisations of the past.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:55.600
This classical look was based on the buildings of ancient Greece and Rome, a reminder of a world of strength and order.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:04.400
The entrance to the palace is another striking example of this passionate love affair with the ancient civilisations of Greece and Rome.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:24.920
This huge portico, these high columns and, at the top, a vast triangular stone roof, and then the doorway here, high, narrow, the kind of doorway that even a conquering hero would feel humble to go through.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:44.480
When you get inside, you really realise this isn't a cosy little home, even for the grandest family.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:54.240
This is a glorious monument, this great hall with its triumphal arch dedicated to Queen Anne and Marlborough himself.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:05:01.800
And in the ceiling, this vast painting of the duke of Marlborough with the battle plan for Blenheim, laying it before Britannia.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:18.640
These 18th century Britons are painted to look like the classical gods and goddesses, reliving heroic myths of the past.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:25.440
I don't suppose he'd read even half these books, but it's the look of the library that matters.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:36.520
It's a temple of learning, fit for an age that believed that, in place of chaos, their world could be ordered, improved...and measured.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.320
Blenheim boasts 18 intricate and valuable clocks dating back to the 1700s.

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:55.480
How do you know which clock this is?

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:00.400
Well, this is from the green drawing room so...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.800
Green left, green right, green centre.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.840
Two more keys!

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.480
I think we've found it.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:26.640
You're obviously very interested in time.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.120
Are you very a precise person?

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:27.560
Absolutely, oh, yes, indeed.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.040
Yes, absolutely, yes, in every way.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.560
If I'm going to meet somebody at 7.15, it's exactly 7.15, and that's it.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:41.720
And I get a bit irritated if people don't turn up at the right time.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:42.320
You must be a nightmare to live with.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:43.480
Must be!

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.080
What's it like working in a place like this?

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:45.800
Marvellous.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:46.760
Is it?

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.080
Oh, it's just wonderful.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:58.080
In this particular room, I've very often worked on a winter afternoon, you get the impression that the people in these portraits are watching.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.600
Would you like to live here?

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:02.880
Wouldn't like to pay the heating bill.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:05.400
But you'd have to be a duke, would you like to be a duke?

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.000
Ah, well, yes, that wouldn't be too bad.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:09.800
I think everybody should be a duke, myself.

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:12.360
That would be wonderful.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:14.160
Live in a house like this.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:14.600
Yes.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:19.160
Blenheim is a bold statement in a grand, new style.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:27.880
We'd go on building in this new classical manner for over 100 years, right through the Georgian age, named after the kings that ruled Britain in the 18th century.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:32.800
Over time, the look would become refined and simplified.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:40.600
And it was seen not just in country houses but in towns and cities, and even in gardens.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:50.720
I drove south from Blenheim, into Wiltshire, to see how they did it.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:27.200
What could be more natural than to go for a tranquil row on a lake in the middle of the English countryside?

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:31.720
And yet there's nothing natural at all about where I am.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.480
This is a man-made landscape.

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:42.520
This is designed to be beautiful and created by man, not by nature.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:53.120
The gardens of Stourhead were laid out in the 1740s by a wealthy banker called Henry Hoare.

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:58.640
This is a vision of paradise brought to life.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:03.440
A scene of natural beauty, improved by man to make it perfect.

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:09.960
There's a path all the way round the lake.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:15.760
And at various points on it, there are classical temples, temples based on Greek and Roman myths.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:29.400
But the clever thing is you can be walking along the path like this, just the lake and the ducks, and then suddenly round a corner and a temple bursts into view.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:44.400
So the temple makes the countryside more exciting, more vivid, punctuates the scenery for you, making the landscape dramatic.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:56.200
Epic tales of Greek gods and Roman heroes fascinated the Georgians.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:06.440
Rich young men flocked to Italy, on what they called the grand tour, to steep themselves in the culture that they so admired.

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:15.280
And on their return, they created, in their own corner of England, a version of what they'd seen abroad.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:29.800
Inside his copy of the Pantheon in ancient Rome, Henry Hoare placed his collection of statues of the gods and goddesses of myth.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:49.720
This great statue is at the heart of the temple - marble figure of Hercules, doing the first of his 12 labours, which was killing the lion.

00:10:50.920 --> 00:11:08.040
And the sculptor took the dimensions of it from an ancient classical statue but, to get the detail right, he got a famous prize fighter, Jack Broughton, to model the biceps.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:23.160
Georgian land owners like Henry Hoare believed that they could perfect nature, and in so doing, they created some of our finest landscapes.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:41.520
If the classical dream of perfection could be realised in a country house or in a garden, so it could in a city.

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:51.000
25 miles from the peace of Stourhead lies the grandest Georgian city of all, Bath.

00:11:51.640 --> 00:12:01.360
At the beginning of the 18th century, Bath was still a small old-fashioned rural town, contained within its medieval walls.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:06.600
Water ran through open sewers down unlit streets.

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:19.160
The hot spa water had attracted people to Bath since before Roman times, and when royal visits made the city fashionable, the elite started flocking here to take the waters.

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:27.560
The daily social round started with a dip at the baths.

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:49.320
People have been swimming in these... warm spa waters for hundreds of years.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:13:05.880
In the 18th century, men would swim in britches and white cotton smocks and tied by a rope to the side so that if they got engaged in a saucy conversation, they wouldn't float away in the middle of it.

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:10.520
But the thing the 18th century introduced was actually to drink the spa water.

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:13.120
And it's meant to be very good for all sorts of ailments.

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:21.560
It smells slightly sulphurous, a little bad...bad egg smell.

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:27.680
It does taste like tepid bath water, to tell the truth.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:32.880
I'm fully restored.

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:44.600
Anyone who was anyone came to Bath.

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:54.360
In 1728, a start was made on creating a glittering new Georgian city - orderly and refined.

00:13:55.200 --> 00:14:07.760
It became Georgian England's most glamorous resort, called the Valley of Pleasure, complete with meeting rooms, coffee shops, costumiers and coiffeurs.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:13.240
The whole secret of Bath was that it should be elegant.

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:23.520
They even advertised that the city had pavements to walk on so you didn't get splashed by mud, and you could walk along here, talking and seeing and being seen.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:30.280
But the key to elegance was to provide housing for people, so that they could live in style.

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:36.320
It was a great British invention, the terrace house, that allowed this to happen.

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:37.920
It was a simple idea.

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:42.680
A row of houses joined together in a uniform line, each with its own front door.

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:50.440
Terraces could be copied and repeated, bringing the discipline of regularity and structure and order.

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:09.240
The architecture of Bath was the vision of an ambitious developer, John Wood, who modelled it on ancient Rome.

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:17.920
He wanted to catch the mood of the glories of the past and make them the wonder of modern Britain.

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:33.200
This must be one of the grandest set of terraces in Bath, the Circus, built almost like a wedding cake - tiers of columns one on top of the other, and a great balustrade with acorns running round.

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:35.960
It's actually three big terraces that swirl round to make this circus.

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:42.400
And as you walk round, you kind of go dizzy with excitement looking at it.

00:15:49.360 --> 00:16:07.840
Britain's first circular street of houses was so striking, its curved tiers of columns so imposing that some visitors compared it to the great amphitheatre of the Colosseum in Rome.

00:16:18.200 --> 00:16:25.040
But the uniformity of these crescents was only skin deep.

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:29.480
A view round the back tells a different story.

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:42.200
Compared with the smooth front of the Circus, the back of these houses is a bit of a jumble.

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:50.800
Very grand, but still a jumble, some curved, some angular.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:17:01.800
And the reason for that is that Wood did the whole design of the circus, rented all the land but then gave individual builders the right to build as they chose.

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:09.640
So every builder built a different house behind what looks like a uniform facade.

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:24.079
The elegant designs begun at the Circus were developed on a much grander scale.

00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:29.640
Bath's Royal Crescent.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:37.760
Here, the splendour of life in ancient Rome had finally come to town.

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:52.000
Dominating the hilltop with a bold, simple sweep of stone was something people had never seen before.

00:17:57.800 --> 00:18:03.120
When we want to cram a lot of people into a small space, we build vertically.

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:08.520
The Georgians did it differently - they squeezed them in horizontally.

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:15.680
And the result is this magnificent crescent - 30 different homes with 114 pillars dividing them.

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:16.920
And what a perfect place to live.

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:20.360
You look out over this great sweep of open countryside.

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:25.000
And just a short walk away down there, all the pleasures of the city.

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:27.760
Absolute magic.

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:42.880
The fashionable way to get about town was by the Georgian equivalent of the taxi, the sedan chair.

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:58.400
They had number plates, taxi ranks and set fees, and they caused traffic jams.

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:12.520
The King's daughter Princess Amelia once insisted on travelling to Bath, from London, by sedan chair.

00:19:11.800 --> 00:19:23.800
Having been pampered and paraded around the city, you headed for the Assembly Rooms, and the evening's entertainment.

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:28.960
There is no modern equivalent of the Bath Assembly Rooms.

00:19:28.640 --> 00:19:30.880
This was a place where everybody doing the season in Bath would come.

00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:35.080
As long as you paid your money, you could come in here and be treated as an equal.

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:41.880
You'd come and play cards, sip tea, talk.

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:48.440
And then if you felt like it, come through into this magnificent room, the ballroom.

00:19:47.680 --> 00:20:05.880
Just imagine it, the rustling of silk, sparkling jewels, the chandeliers gleaming, a thousand or more people, dancing and music, people gossiping and flirting.

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:15.720
There must have been, every night here during the season, a real buzz a real sense of excitement and thrill to be here.

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:24.520
This is a gargantuan job.

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:32.360
It takes a day if I was to do it on my own.

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:36.600
You need a dishwasher.

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:37.600
I'll wash and you dry.

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:41.520
These actually cost £100 each when they were first...

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:42.000
Each chandelier?

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:43.480
What are they worth now?

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:48.080
£500,000 each.

00:20:47.240 --> 00:20:49.920
Have you ever known any accidents happen?

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:58.120
Um, I think in the last 15 years, a judge threw a Savoy cabbage into the air and broke one.

00:20:57.280 --> 00:20:59.560
A judge threw a Savoy cabbage at...?

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:08.280
Yeah, it must have been an ornamental one on the table and maybe he wasn't as sober as a judge.

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:12.960
Drunk as a lord, drunk as a judge.

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:25.800
The building boom led by Bath was only one aspect of Georgian enterprise.

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:31.880
Away from the sophisticated cities, another Britain was being built.

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:39.920
The quiet landscape of the West Country was being transformed by the energy of a Georgian revolution.

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:49.800
Across fields, valleys and hills, a new transport network was being carved.

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:53.520
The canals.

00:21:52.680 --> 00:22:00.800
The Kennet and Avon Canal was dug to connect Bath and Bristol in the west to London.

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:07.080
It cut out weeks of inconvenient travel by land or sea.

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:18.400
It was now possible, for the first time, to move raw materials and manufactured goods from city to city by horse-drawn barge.

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:22.880
This is Bonnie, she's 20 years old.

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:29.920
She spends the whole summer going up and down the canal taking passengers and then winter time she goes out in the field and has a rest.

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:33.880
And she's a very, very good horse.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:43.680
And it's quite a skill, cos it's a heavy barge, and she mustn't slip and she mustn't fall into the canal.

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:59.480
How long would it take you to get from, say, the docks at Bristol, how far up would you go?

00:23:01.480 --> 00:23:06.280
There was a big trading base built up, a little bit further on, to and from Devizes.

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:13.200
A lot of the cargoes came in from Bristol, about a 4-day journey with a horse, or 2 horses when they were full.

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:13.760
One horse if the barge was empty.

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:15.440
Two horses?

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:15.560
Two horses with a fully laden barge.

00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:19.400
And one horse when it was empty?

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:20.320
That's right.

00:23:20.120 --> 00:23:21.600
What did they do with the other horse?

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:26.640
They would put the horse on the deck, or they'd walk it alongside the barge, but it wouldn't be working.

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:31.160
Did they go day and night?

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:34.280
They went day and night and they kept going.

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:36.480
And would they have been a husband and wife team?

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:39.880
They were doing what was relatively short...

00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:46.200
I'd be at home in my cottage in Honey Street.

00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:52.080
Georgian canal builders were determined to take control of nature.

00:23:51.600 --> 00:24:04.360
If they met a hill, they either tunnelled through it, or took the canal over the top, with a flight of locks.

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:08.840
One of the most spectacular flights ever built is at Caen Hill near Devizes.

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:10.080
A staircase of water.

00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:21.600
29 locks in all were needed to lift shipping out of the Avon valley up onto the hills north of Salisbury Plain.

00:24:21.560 --> 00:24:30.600
The canal had to climb so far above sea level that there was no natural source of water.

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:38.640
To pump water where it was needed, the engineers had an ingenious solution.

00:24:39.040 --> 00:24:40.160
Steam.

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:50.320
Steam power was the greatest technological leap forward of the Georgian age.

00:24:54.400 --> 00:25:04.960
And the most important building on the whole canal was this small structure, Crofton pumping station, built in 1807.

00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:17.920
Its crucial job was to pump water into the canal so that boats could float up the flight of locks and over the hill.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:39.200
The building had to be strong enough to support the weight of all the machinery inside and, at the top, the gigantic beam weighing six tons.

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:55.280
That massive machinery, that brilliant harnessing of power, is all designed just to bring water up this pipe, cast iron, also 200 years old.

00:25:55.760 --> 00:26:11.480
And it pushes the water up, five tons fill this pipe and a ton at a time are pushed off, out of the top.

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:16.240
And to see where it all comes from, I've got to go down here.

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:32.080
And this is where the water came from, if I can squeeze past here.

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:44.720
From the very bottom of the well down there, and it rose 12 metres, that's 40 feet, to the top.

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:46.800
Now 40 feet may not seem very much to us.

00:26:46.080 --> 00:26:54.640
But it was the ability to move water that 40 feet that allowed London to be connected to the West Country by canal.

00:26:54.080 --> 00:27:03.960
As a result of which, industry grew, people prospered.

00:27:05.720 --> 00:27:11.000
In the west, the waterways led to one of Britain's richest cities, Bristol.

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:18.400
Where Bath was about pleasure, Bristol, only a dozen miles away, was about making money.

00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:24.480
At the heart of the city was a busy trading port.

00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:44.360
It was this harbour and the docks and the canals that fed into it that were at the heart of Bristol's commercial success.

00:27:43.600 --> 00:27:49.680
In the 1700s, there were said to be 3,000 boats registered to the port of Bristol.

00:27:49.640 --> 00:27:55.480
Boats would have been filling this space, the masts towering above the houses.

00:27:55.000 --> 00:28:01.440
And the boats themselves had to be very carefully moored so they didn't bang into each other in this tiny space.

00:28:00.680 --> 00:28:09.280
Shipshape and Bristol fashion, it was called.

00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:14.200
Fortunes were made from global trade and Bristol's merchants grew rich.

00:28:14.200 --> 00:28:20.080
Streets of new homes sprang up above the harbour.

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:31.000
This house belonged to John Pinney, who owned plantations in the West Indies.

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:36.680
It's one of many houses built by a new generation of middle-class Georgians who wanted comfort and the latest conveniences.

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:45.280
This is what John Pinney built.

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:56.280
Like every successful businessman, even today, he wanted to show his success.

00:28:59.760 --> 00:29:08.680
The dining room, or eating room, as he called it.

00:29:16.960 --> 00:29:28.000
And the table laid out for cheese and dessert, when the servants would have withdrawn.

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:38.760
And this magnificent cheese container, a box, which rolls on castors, so you can pass it down the table.

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:47.480
Lift the lid off, and it's metal lined, with little holes for the cheese to breathe.

00:29:49.400 --> 00:29:53.960
There were two ways of calling the servants, there's the... simple ringing the bell.

00:29:57.080 --> 00:30:08.200
Or a speaking tube, you blow down, and it makes a whistle sound in the kitchen, and then you say, "Cook, could you send up more gravy for the mutton, Cook?

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:10.280
"Hello, is anyone down there?"

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:17.360
And Mrs Bridges or whoever would get the extra gravy, take it in here.

00:30:16.520 --> 00:30:22.840
A lift, the dumb waiter, pull the handle, up it will go, to be served upstairs.

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:25.800
And she'd be saying, "Oh, them upstairs never stop asking for things."

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:38.320
These days, every multimillionaire wants to have a swimming pool in the basement, the ultimate luxury.

00:30:38.360 --> 00:30:42.160
Well, Pinney had the Georgian equivalent.

00:30:41.520 --> 00:30:51.920
In the basement he built this stone plunge pool, filled it with cold water, and says he used to swim every morning, and it kept him healthy.

00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:52.960
Brr!

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:54.640
Rather him than me.

00:30:57.960 --> 00:31:02.760
The population of Bristol tripled in the boom years of the 18th century.

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:10.040
Nearby villages, like Clifton, were transformed into fine suburbs.

00:31:10.960 --> 00:31:23.480
But for all their respectable looks, many of the Georgian houses of Bristol, John Pinney's among them, were built on a very unrespectable trade.

00:31:26.280 --> 00:31:33.160
By 1730, the city had become the biggest slave trading port in the country.

00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:42.400
A third of all British slaving ships used it as their home port.

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:54.160
They shipped goods from Britain to Africa, to exchange for slaves who were shipped to America and the West Indies to work the plantations.

00:31:53.240 --> 00:32:00.280
The ships sailed home to Bristol laden with cotton, tobacco and sugar.

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:17.160
Slave traders and plantation owners lined their pockets and furnished their homes on the toil of men and women, many of whom laboured to their deaths.

00:32:22.520 --> 00:32:27.440
But not everyone accepted the slave trade as normal.

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:29.720
Some felt it was shameful.

00:32:28.960 --> 00:32:42.960
Hidden among the department stores of Bristol is a place that was built by one of the most ardent anti-slavery campaigners.

00:32:49.920 --> 00:33:14.720
This is the first Methodist chapel built in Britain, designed by the founder of Methodism, John Wesley, as a place to bring in the people who wouldn't come to the established church, people who wanted to hear his passionate words about religion and God and who'd listen to him in the open air, in their thousands,4 and had here a home they could come to, to listen to him preach.

00:33:14.080 --> 00:33:24.040
In true Georgian style, Wesley's "new room", as he called it, was simple and understated.

00:33:25.160 --> 00:33:28.840
Six plain stone columns supported the ceiling.

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:34.880
And light flooded in from a plain lantern window above.

00:33:37.680 --> 00:33:47.560
Wesley's outspoken preaching often sparked uproar from the congregation, and he had to be kept at arms length from them, for his own safety.

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:58.800
So there were two pulpits - the upper one for preaching, the lower one for reading - and neither could be reached from the pews on the ground floor.

00:33:58.080 --> 00:34:05.040
Whenever Wesley spoke, he attracted controversy.

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:16.000
They didn't put windows round the bottom of this chapel for fear the mob would break them and, instead, they had this great lantern with light flooding down into the chapel from high up, which had one other odd advantage.

00:34:15.480 --> 00:34:24.120
There's a little window there, leading into the rooms there, where Wesley could stand and keep an eye on whoever was preaching here in the pulpit.

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:29.480
Wesley was a passionate opponent of the evils of slavery.

00:34:28.760 --> 00:34:37.320
When he was in his 80s, he came to this chapel in Bristol and announced he was going to speak about the wickedness and call for its abolition.

00:34:36.520 --> 00:34:44.120
A huge congregation came, some of them people in favour of abolition, some of them, of course, this being Bristol, in favour of keeping slavery.

00:34:45.040 --> 00:34:52.400
And he used the vehement language he'd always used, "What's the justice of murdering thousands of people in their own land?

00:34:51.719 --> 00:35:05.080
"What's the justice of putting thousands of people on ships, casting them like dung into the sea, "and tens of thousands into cruel slavery, to which they're so unjustly reduced?"

00:35:04.440 --> 00:35:06.520
And he described how everybody listened, enraptured.

00:35:06.080 --> 00:35:14.840
And suddenly, in the middle of the sermon, there was a violent explosion, and everybody started fighting each other here.

00:35:14.160 --> 00:35:19.920
And the benches were broken and there was noise and confusion, and he said it was like lightning struck the place.

00:35:19.120 --> 00:35:25.680
And then after six minutes it suddenly stopped, and he went on with his sermon.

00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:34.160
Wesley fought for the abolition of slavery until the very end.

00:35:33.400 --> 00:35:37.280
He wrote one final campaign letter from his death bed.

00:35:37.160 --> 00:35:45.960
16 years later, in 1807, when abolition became law, his prayers were finally answered.

00:35:54.400 --> 00:35:59.480
Beyond Bristol, other profound changes were under way.

00:36:01.280 --> 00:36:21.760
I headed deep into the West Country, crossing the empty sweep of Dartmoor, toward the very tip of England, to see the first stirrings of an industrial revolution which would radically change the way we lived and the way we worked.

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:59.400
This coastline is one of the richest in the world.

00:36:59.080 --> 00:37:04.080
Almost every mineral can be found just along this stretch.

00:37:04.880 --> 00:37:16.160
And for hundreds of years, the Cornish have mined here, looking mainly for tin and copper, cutting into the rock face of the cliff, down, following the seams right out under the sea.

00:37:17.320 --> 00:37:24.960
And the only problem they had was the further they went, the deeper they cut, the bigger the shaft, the greater the problem of flooding.

00:37:25.120 --> 00:37:37.640
And all the water had to be taken out, pumped out by hand, until these buildings came to the rescue.

00:37:38.440 --> 00:37:50.200
Clinging to the rocks at Botallack on the north Cornish coast are these engine houses, now ruined, known as the Crowns.

00:37:50.000 --> 00:37:54.120
They were built to contain the newly-designed steam-driven engines which pumped out the water.

00:37:54.000 --> 00:37:59.400
Miners could now dig deeper than they'd ever been before.

00:37:59.320 --> 00:38:08.360
Families moved to Cornwall from all over Britain, in search of work in the tin and copper mines.

00:38:08.680 --> 00:38:16.560
But what they found when they got there was often hot, damp and dangerous.

00:38:25.240 --> 00:38:32.880
It was a tough life being a miner - it wasn't just the narrowness of these walls.

00:38:32.000 --> 00:38:38.560
They had very little light, just a candle on the helmet here, and very little air too.

00:38:39.920 --> 00:38:53.880
And sometimes when they were under the sea, it was said if they got the shaft very close to the sea bed, they could actually hear the boulders in a storm, rocking against each other up above.

00:38:52.920 --> 00:39:00.040
And then sea water would seep through, bringing chemicals from the rocks, which could burn your skin.

00:38:59.320 --> 00:39:08.000
And then, of course, there was always the danger of a rock fall and a sudden death.

00:39:20.360 --> 00:39:21.960
Ah, this is what they were after.

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:36.480
Here, gleaming gold and blue - the ore, with all the precious minerals in it, running like the filling in a sandwich from right up there, 800 feet away down below me.

00:39:53.600 --> 00:40:03.320
And this is what was left when they'd mined all the ore, this huge cavern reaching up and right down behind me there.

00:40:03.880 --> 00:40:08.280
And these tree trunks were put in to hold the two apart, the roof and the floor.

00:40:08.960 --> 00:40:14.040
Like pillars in a temple.

00:40:14.440 --> 00:40:24.480
And when sometimes the earth moved, or there was a small earthquake, they could come back down and find these tree trunks had been crushed, as though they were just matchsticks.

00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:35.800
Is this how they used to mine?

00:40:34.960 --> 00:40:36.240
Yes.

00:40:39.320 --> 00:40:40.480
What's the technique?

00:40:39.680 --> 00:40:44.880
Well, basically, you've got a small steel, with a sharpened chisel point.

00:40:44.080 --> 00:40:50.800
You place it against the face, you beat the end, and each time you hit it, you turn it slightly.

00:40:50.000 --> 00:40:58.760
It was quite hazardous, especially working by candlelight, and it's not uncommon for fathers and sons to work together in a team.

00:40:58.840 --> 00:41:07.920
And there have been documented cases where the father's missed the end of the drill steel with a sledgehammer and hit his son in the head and killed him.

00:41:07.880 --> 00:41:09.600
Oh, horrible.

00:41:08.840 --> 00:41:11.400
In the dark...?

00:41:10.760 --> 00:41:16.560
Well, at the moment we've got lights, they were working by candlelight.

00:41:15.680 --> 00:41:17.360
They had to buy their candles from the mine.

00:41:16.520 --> 00:41:25.680
So, because they had to pay for them, they only used the absolute minimum candlelight they could get away with.

00:41:24.840 --> 00:41:29.680
What a horrible story.

00:41:36.040 --> 00:41:49.480
There was a boom in mining - coal was needed for heating homes, for driving the furnaces to produce steam, iron was in demand for building bridges.

00:41:49.040 --> 00:41:57.920
Copper, to turn into brass, to make the things people wanted to buy - doorknobs and bedsteads.

00:41:57.080 --> 00:41:59.480
It was in a way the beginning of a kind of consumer society.

00:42:00.240 --> 00:42:09.480
New ports like Charlestown, near St Austell, were built to carry on the trade.

00:42:08.640 --> 00:42:11.440
Manufactured good were now being shipped all over the country.

00:42:11.320 --> 00:42:16.720
It was the beginning of a new age of comfort - carpets and curtains and wall papers.

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:21.400
Houses were being decorated with the first mass-produced furnishings.

00:42:25.440 --> 00:42:37.000
Saltram House, near Plymouth, was redesigned in the 1740s for the local squire, John Parker.

00:42:36.320 --> 00:42:45.800
The outside reveals the trends in Georgian house building.

00:42:44.920 --> 00:42:49.400
Symmetrical, in true classical style, but plainer and simpler.

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:52.160
The contrast with the interior is breathtaking.

00:43:11.360 --> 00:43:16.240
Just look at this room, this is the great drawing room, or the saloon.

00:43:17.400 --> 00:43:29.640
The first thing you notice, this huge, very fine plaster ceiling, and below it a vast carpet that seems almost to mirror the ceiling.

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:41.600
The carpet was made just up the road, Axminster, cost £126, worth thousands and thousands now.

00:43:41.480 --> 00:43:52.160
The Parkers wanted the latest thing in interior design and they employed the most famous designer of the day to make sure they got it.

00:43:51.200 --> 00:44:04.080
Robert Adam took charge of the look of the house and chose everything, from its plaster covings to its gilded furniture.

00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:12.360
In the hallway, he used exotic touches of mahogany.

00:44:12.600 --> 00:44:22.200
And up above, another highly-prized feature - a delicate plaster ceiling, in the newly fashionable colour, off-white.

00:44:34.160 --> 00:44:39.400
At the top of the stairs was a succession of finely furnished bedrooms.

00:44:48.440 --> 00:44:54.720
There was a great passion for wallpaper, and this paper was actually imported from China.

00:44:57.920 --> 00:45:03.160
And it shows another of their passions, or fashions - the fashion for drinking tea.

00:45:05.720 --> 00:45:11.680
Here's the tea being put out and dried under a roof there, ready to be transported.

00:45:15.760 --> 00:45:20.720
This is, um, three, four workmen actually making tea chests.

00:45:20.480 --> 00:45:27.560
Up here, they're actually filling the boxes, it looks like people standing in coffins, but they're treading the tea down into the boxes.

00:45:29.800 --> 00:45:33.760
Now where are they tasting it?

00:45:32.800 --> 00:45:33.960
Somewhere they'll be tasting it.

00:45:37.320 --> 00:45:42.040
I can't quite see how...

00:45:41.880 --> 00:45:46.640
Yes, there they're tasting it, the little teapot, two cups, he's just trying it out.

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:54.400
This tea was grown, it had to be harvested first of all.

00:45:53.440 --> 00:46:00.800
From there they go over the mountains, it would take six weeks' journey, down to the sea port.

00:45:59.880 --> 00:46:06.560
There the European tea traders would look at the tea, choose the best tea, and start shipping it to England.

00:46:05.720 --> 00:46:14.280
The whole process took over a year before the Georgians here could sit down to the latest fashion - a nice cup of tea.

00:46:19.400 --> 00:46:20.880
That's very delicate.

00:46:20.000 --> 00:46:33.760
We dust it with a brush because the dust will sit in around... any little edges, and because it's gilded, it's fine gilded, the dust will just rub the gold off.

00:46:32.800 --> 00:46:36.160
It must be worth a fortune, this.

00:46:35.280 --> 00:46:38.800
I believe so, but I don't know.

00:46:37.880 --> 00:46:39.280
I'd rather not know!

00:46:38.480 --> 00:46:41.800
Why would you rather not know?

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:44.400
Well, it make you a little bit more nervous of touching the pieces.

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:47.920
So you have to approach it with a bit of confidence and not be afraid to move it around.

00:46:47.440 --> 00:46:50.560
Ever broken anything?

00:46:49.640 --> 00:46:50.800
Yes.

00:46:50.200 --> 00:46:51.280
Have you?

00:46:51.480 --> 00:46:54.960
I've broken the slop bowl to this set.

00:46:54.000 --> 00:46:55.360
What did they say?

00:46:54.600 --> 00:46:56.240
Nothing yet.

00:46:57.000 --> 00:47:00.000
Accidents will happen.

00:46:59.080 --> 00:47:03.880
In 15 years, I've broken two pieces.

00:47:04.720 --> 00:47:05.960
Very pale.

00:47:06.800 --> 00:47:09.520
Cream?

00:47:11.160 --> 00:47:13.120
And they would also have had sugar.

00:47:15.680 --> 00:47:20.040
Well, I don't drink my tea like this.

00:47:19.240 --> 00:47:20.720
I don't drink green tea.

00:47:20.160 --> 00:47:21.280
And brown sugar!

00:47:21.240 --> 00:47:24.280
I have to use these.

00:47:25.520 --> 00:47:27.560
Easier said than done.

00:47:29.800 --> 00:47:33.640
It's looking like soup now.

00:47:34.280 --> 00:47:35.400
It is.

00:47:34.520 --> 00:47:39.520
And you would have stirred your tea without clanking your spoon.

00:47:41.720 --> 00:47:45.960
Now, I bet we don't say cheers.

00:47:45.960 --> 00:47:47.120
We will.

00:47:46.160 --> 00:47:54.440
If you're playing the host, the lady of the house, I don't think you'd say cheers in 1780 or wherever we are.

00:47:53.600 --> 00:47:55.600
We can say it anyway.

00:47:57.120 --> 00:48:00.000
Ah!

00:47:59.240 --> 00:48:02.040
That's better.

00:48:10.160 --> 00:48:13.360
There was another revolution taking place in Britain during the Georgian age.

00:48:12.560 --> 00:48:19.640
It wasn't as dramatic as the use of steam power or the building of canals, but its impact was just as profound.

00:48:19.360 --> 00:48:22.720
It was road building.

00:48:23.120 --> 00:48:35.960
For the final part of my journey, I'm heading north-west into Wales, and on to Ireland, at that time united with Britain, to see how the Georgians transformed the way we travelled.

00:48:37.320 --> 00:48:42.800
Up till then, Britain's road system hadn't been much more than a collection of muddy tracks.

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:49.080
But beginning in the 1730s, a new network of toll roads was built.

00:48:48.200 --> 00:48:58.440
Money was collected from passing travellers and went to maintaining and expanding the new roads.

00:48:58.960 --> 00:49:03.960
Every few miles, tiny pepper-pot-shaped toll houses were built.

00:49:03.880 --> 00:49:08.560
Each had a gate or turnpike so that fees could be collected.

00:49:08.120 --> 00:49:11.920
The prices were written up outside.

00:49:11.280 --> 00:49:22.480
A horse was a penny, a coach or carriage was four pence, and most expensive, at ten pence, was a score of cattle.

00:49:24.960 --> 00:49:28.080
Can I come in?

00:49:28.120 --> 00:49:29.840
Oh, it is very cosy, isn't it?

00:49:32.720 --> 00:49:34.680
How big is it?

00:49:33.800 --> 00:49:36.560
About eight feet across?

00:49:36.360 --> 00:49:38.280
I've no idea.

00:49:38.560 --> 00:49:42.800
I know it's easy to decorate - I used to decorate.

00:49:42.040 --> 00:49:43.520
You can reach the ceiling.

00:49:43.720 --> 00:49:45.480
So, what was the idea?

00:49:44.680 --> 00:49:46.880
You have a window all the way round?

00:49:46.160 --> 00:49:51.160
Well, he could watch the people, the travellers coming.

00:49:50.960 --> 00:49:53.640
But aren't you a bit disturbed by all the cars?

00:49:53.760 --> 00:50:02.240
In the 30 years I've been here, I've had ten cars and wagons through the wall.

00:50:01.400 --> 00:50:04.440
I wouldn't dare sit here at night with lorries thundering past.

00:50:03.680 --> 00:50:08.240
My cousin was staying with me one year, and it was the day for going home.

00:50:08.240 --> 00:50:10.880
She was sitting here.

00:50:09.920 --> 00:50:13.600
She said, "What's it like when a car hits?"

00:50:12.920 --> 00:50:15.400
Two minutes later, a car hit.

00:50:14.760 --> 00:50:17.800
She was white, so was I.

00:50:16.960 --> 00:50:22.480
She said, "Oh, I'll go home tomorrow."

00:50:45.480 --> 00:51:02.560
The road I'm driving along was built for a special purpose - to speed up the journey from London to Dublin, and it went right across the most perilous bit of the mountains of Snowdonia, this beautiful countryside in Wales.

00:51:02.120 --> 00:51:09.280
They needed to travel backwards and forwards with letters and people and they just couldn't do it unless a proper road was built.

00:51:12.520 --> 00:51:25.080
When the government of Ireland was united with Great Britain's in 1801, a new long-distance road to Ireland became essential.

00:51:24.400 --> 00:51:35.640
The man chosen to built this ambitious road was one of Britain's most famous engineers - Thomas Telford.

00:51:39.360 --> 00:51:51.840
One feature of Telford's grand new road became his most celebrated achievement - the Menai suspension bridge.

00:51:51.000 --> 00:52:11.840
100 feet above the dangerous waters of the Menai Straits, graceful stone arches bear chains of cast iron, which carry the roadway across to Anglesey and on to the ferry port at Holyhead.

00:52:16.040 --> 00:52:32.680
It was a fantastic achievement, really exciting, and they celebrated every stage of the building within one year - when they first put a chain across, vicars came out and gave their blessing, and three workmen actually walked across on a chain.

00:52:31.920 --> 00:52:38.440
When the final chains were in place, they lowered a band and played the national anthem.

00:52:38.120 --> 00:52:45.560
When the first footpath across was created, they fired a 21-gun salute.

00:52:44.680 --> 00:52:52.480
And then, when the whole bridge was complete, 5,000 people came on foot, to watch the bridge opening and horse-drawn carriages going across for the first time.

00:52:52.800 --> 00:52:59.640
There were so many people, Telford said they mustn't go across cos it might risk the bridge.

00:52:59.600 --> 00:53:12.600
After centuries of treacherous ferry crossings, there was now a safe route to Anglesey and, from there, onwards to Ireland.

00:53:20.240 --> 00:53:22.160
Dublin.

00:53:25.120 --> 00:53:29.200
In the 18th century, a city to rival London.

00:53:28.560 --> 00:53:33.760
Even today, it's dominated by landmarks from the Georgian age.

00:53:32.920 --> 00:53:38.800
The Custom House, with its monumental facades.

00:53:38.400 --> 00:53:45.920
The imposing domed drum shape of the legal building, the Four Courts.

00:53:44.960 --> 00:53:55.520
And the Bank of Ireland with its imposing columns, the grand entrance to what was once the Irish houses of parliament.

00:53:54.560 --> 00:54:04.840
Dublin was transformed in the 1750s, when new quarters of the city were developed with wide terraced streets and elegant squares.

00:54:04.320 --> 00:54:12.400
The houses were built with a typical Georgian care for regularity and uniformity.

00:54:12.520 --> 00:54:20.160
Exterior decoration was kept to a minimum, except for one feature.

00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:27.080
The only little luxury they allowed themselves, the only extravagant gesture, was in the doorway to the house itself.

00:54:27.080 --> 00:54:36.480
And this wonderful fanlight, of which there were hundreds and hundreds in Dublin, all made of cast iron like that.

00:54:35.960 --> 00:54:41.520
They were needed of course to let light in, but look how beautifully decorated they are.

00:54:50.440 --> 00:54:53.520
But a shadow was to fall on this elegant world.

00:54:58.160 --> 00:55:09.560
The city had been built up as a rival to London, the seat of Irish government.

00:55:10.360 --> 00:55:17.200
When that moved to London, with the union, the elite moved with it.

00:55:16.600 --> 00:55:21.800
And their grand homes went into decline.

00:55:21.600 --> 00:55:28.960
The fine houses of Henrietta Street would never be the same.

00:55:46.960 --> 00:55:52.920
It's one of the oldest and grandest houses in Henrietta Street, built in 1743.

00:55:52.160 --> 00:55:57.720
It was lived in, first of all by a peer of the realm, then by a succession of bishops.

00:55:56.840 --> 00:55:59.960
And then it fell on hard times.

00:56:01.560 --> 00:56:08.160
As the better-off abandoned their fine houses, the poor moved in.

00:56:07.720 --> 00:56:14.280
Henrietta Street turned from a wealthy architectural showpiece into something quite different.

00:56:25.800 --> 00:56:32.520
Like so many of these grand houses, this one fell into terrible decline.

00:56:32.680 --> 00:56:40.320
First of all, just not looked after properly, and then, finally, subdivided into scores of little residences.

00:56:40.640 --> 00:56:46.240
It's worked out that in this street there were up to 100 people living in every house.

00:56:46.520 --> 00:56:48.640
And you can see how they divided it up.

00:56:48.120 --> 00:56:52.760
There's an old partition mark there.

00:56:52.080 --> 00:56:58.160
And then, where the green paint stops and the purple starts, there was a ceiling right the way across here.

00:56:58.720 --> 00:57:05.360
There's another partition mark down the side there.

00:57:04.640 --> 00:57:07.920
So that bit is one house.

00:57:19.120 --> 00:57:27.240
They removed the staircase entirely and put separate floors in so they could cram in more people.

00:57:26.720 --> 00:57:32.600
So, over 100 people living in a house designed for, what, ten?

00:57:45.720 --> 00:57:55.680
The Georgians had created an elegant sophisticated world for themselves - at least for the elite - one in which order prevailed over chaos.

00:57:56.400 --> 00:58:06.240
But the very fascination that they'd taken in science, the discoveries they'd made, meant that the Industrial Revolution was starting to gather pace.

00:58:05.600 --> 00:58:10.000
And as it did, the facade of the Georgian world, like these terrace houses, began to crumble.

00:58:11.560 --> 00:58:23.600
By the time Queen Victoria came to the throne, industrial revolution was speeding up and social revolution was on the way.

00:58:22.800 --> 00:58:42.320
In the next programme, I'll be travelling to the north of England - the power house of Victorian Britain - to see how the new industrial age brought riches to some, misery to others and dramatic change to all.
